##DESCRIPTION
##  Algebra problem: Rewrite expression without using negative exponents
##ENDDESCRIPTION

##KEYWORDS('algebra', 'exponent', 'fraction')

## DBsubject(Algebra)
## DBchapter(Properties of exponents, rational exponents and radicals)
## DBsection(Properties of exponents)
## Level(2)
## Author(K. Andrew Parker)
## Institution(CUNY City Tech)
## Language(en)

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros(
    'PGstandard.pl',    # Standard macros for PG language
    'PGML.pl',
    'contextFraction.pl',
    'PGcourse.pl'
);


Context("LimitedFraction")->flags->set(
    showMixedNumbers  => 0,
    reduceConstants   => 0,
    allowMixedNumbers => 0
);

$A = random(5, 20);

$ansA = Fraction(1, $A);

$ansB = Compute(random(5, 20));

$B = Fraction(1, $ansB);

$Cnum = Compute(random(7, 19, 2));
$Cden = $Cnum + non_zero_random(-4, 4, 2);

$C    = Fraction($Cnum, $Cden);
$ansC = Fraction($Cden, $Cnum);


BEGIN_PGML

a) Rewrite [` [$A]^{-1} `] without using negative exponents.

    [_]{$ansA}

b) Rewrite [`` \left([$B]\right)^{-1} ``] without using negative exponents.

    [_]{$ansB}

c) Rewrite [`` \left([$C]\right)^{-1} ``] without using negative exponents.

    [_]{$ansC}
END_PGML

#  Answers

ANS($ansA->cmp);
ANS($ansB->cmp);
ANS($ansC->cmp);

BEGIN_PGML_HINT

Recall the property of exponentials: [` B^{-1} \times B^1 = B^{(-1+1)} = B^0 = 1 `]

So you just need to figure out [` what \times B^1 = 1 `]

Hint: reciprocals work really well for this...

END_PGML_HINT
BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION

a) [`` \left([$A]\right)^{-1} = [$ansA]``]

b) [`` \left([$B]\right)^{-1} = [$ansB]``]

c) [``\left([$C]\right)^{-1} = [$ansC]``]

END_PGML_SOLUTION

COMMENT("Funded by US DoE Title V: Opening Gateways grant.");

ENDDOCUMENT();        